.rem The cell writer's default vocabulary: one alias per pica
.rem construct, each painting one row's text in that construct's
.rem ink. The writer wraps and places every line; an alias only
.rem inks. An app replaces this file whole by supplying its own
.rem definitions of the same names to Render.
.rem
.rem Whole-line aliases receive a line that starts at the cursor.
.rem The writer places the title centered on row 0 of panel 0, at
.rem least two cells in, so the bar's background and the title's
.rem foreground each have a blank cell before the first glyph; the
.rem alias paints the bar and the text and never positions.
.def title TEXT
.fg white
.bg blue
.fill 0
$TEXT
.fg
.bg
.enddef
.def byline TEXT
$TEXT
.enddef
.def heading TEXT
.fg yellow
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
.def subheading TEXT
.fg cyan
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
.def tablehead TEXT
.fg cyan
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
.def total TEXT
.fg yellow
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
.def rights TEXT
$TEXT
.enddef
.rem Span aliases overpaint part of a row the writer has already
.rem painted in default ink; the blank cell before the span is the
.rem marker underscore, the term gap, or the column's margin.
.def label TEXT
.fg cyan
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
.def emph TEXT
.fg white
$TEXT
.fg
.enddef
